  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 146 PM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nA weak surface boundary is moving through the area today without \nmuch change to conditions. A ridge builds in behind the boundary \ntonight, which will maintain clear skies and light winds. \nTemperatures stay on the warm side of average for the rest of the \nworkweek with a return of southwesterly low level winds and as an \nupper ridge remains centered just south of the area. Dew points \naround 60 degrees during the afternoon hours will also contribute to \na summer-like feel for now.\n\nThe big weather story late this week and this weekend will be the \ncurrent Tropical Storm Isaias that is strengthening in the Gulf and \nexpected to make landfall near Mobile, AL. The system looks to \nquickly move inland toward the intersection of the Ohio and \nMississippi Rivers. While eastern KS will likely not experience \nimpacts with the system currently expected to stay east of the area, \nrain could occur as close as Columbia or Springfield, MO. Changes to\nour ambient conditions look more probable during the middle of next \nweek as a deep closed low system moves over the southwestern CONUS. \nLong range models continue to show different evolutions of the low, \nhowever, confidence is high on increases POPs next Tuesday and \nWednesday. While there also remains a large amount of spread on \nexpected temperatures during that timeframe, trends do suggest more \nseasonable conditions.",
